&lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"  {t['at']} {t['side']:&amp;lt;4} ${t['usd']:&amp;gt;6,.0f} {t['symbol']} by {t['handle']}"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;__name__&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"__main__"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;argv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Walking through the choices:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Oldest first.&lt;/strong&gt; The feed is newest first, so &lt;code&gt;poll_once&lt;/code&gt; reverses it. You process events in the order they happened.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Save after processing.&lt;/strong&gt; The state file is written once the batch is handled. If the process dies between the two steps, the next run fetches the same batch again and the keys drop what was already seen. The worst case is a replay, never a gap.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Atomic write.&lt;/strong&gt; The state goes to a temporary file and &lt;code&gt;os.replace&lt;/code&gt; swaps it in. A crash during the write leaves the old file or the new one, not half of one.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A bounded memory.&lt;/strong&gt; Only the last 200 keys are kept. The tape holds 30 trades, so 200 is far more than any overlap you can get.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One call a minute.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;INTERVAL&lt;/code&gt; is 60 seconds. The docstring of &lt;code&gt;RateLimitError&lt;/code&gt; says that without a key the limit is about 20 calls a minute per IP, so one a minute is nowhere near it, and it matches the tape: 30 trades kept, and the busy minute below produced 15 new ones.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Backoff.&lt;/strong&gt; On a network error, a 429 or a 5xx, &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt; waits 2 seconds, then 4, 8 and so on up to 120, and gives up after 5 tries. A 4xx other than 429 is a bug in the request, so it is raised at once instead of retried.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Testing the backoff without hitting the API
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I did not want to provoke real errors on a public service, so the backoff is tested with the client's &lt;code&gt;transport&lt;/code&gt; argument, which swaps the network for a function. This one answers 429, then 503, then a valid page, and records the waits instead of sleeping.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# backoff_demo.py: no network. A fake transport fails twice (429, 503) then answers.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;poller&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;copytop&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Client&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;calls&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;iter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;429&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'{"error": "slow down"}'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;503&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'{"error": "unavailable"}'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dumps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"cursor"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"trades"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;encode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;waits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;ct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;max_retries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;transport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;lambda&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;calls&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;answer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sleep&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;waits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;append&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"answered:"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;answer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"cursor"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"after waits"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;waits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Running it for real
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three calls, spread over two processes, to show the cursor and the crash recovery. The first process polls twice, 60 seconds apart. Then it ends, which stands in for a crash, and a second process starts from the file. Here is the output, shortened in the middle (the full file is &lt;code&gt;out.txt&lt;/code&gt;):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;# run 1: first process, 2 iterations (60 s apart)
start, cursor None, 0 keys remembered
poll 1
fetched 30, new 30, duplicates skipped 0, cursor 2026-11-25T23:13:13.312Z
2026-11-25T23:11:33.134Z sell $   230 4kCUKMQJ by pf_3fcb9b3f8f72
2026-11-25T23:11:46.767Z buy  $   400 FoolCoin by pf_9953d6d3879e
2026-11-25T23:11:49.068Z buy  $   422 MEME by pf_d414971aec24
... (25 more trades)
2026-11-25T23:13:12.284Z buy  $   500 PI by pf_084dd8ed340e
2026-11-25T23:13:13.312Z sell $   399 火羊 by pf_84a1b8520cb7
poll 2
fetched 15, new 15, duplicates skipped 0, cursor 2026-11-25T23:13:51.116Z
2026-11-25T23:13:22.295Z buy  $   990 TripleT by pf_6acf0fba83ee
2026-11-25T23:13:22.535Z buy  $   353 PI by pf_6e00b1ebdcc5
2026-11-25T23:13:23.589Z sell $   301 PI by pf_dc9a754ae562
... (10 more trades)
2026-11-25T23:13:45.808Z buy  $   617 RAETO by gabrieIkevin
2026-11-25T23:13:51.116Z sell $   253 BLOB by pf_47a3ecba9fda

# crash: process gone, cursor.json on disk
{"cursor": "2026-11-25T23:13:51.116Z", "seen": ["2026-11-25T23:11:33.134Z|pf_3fcb9b3f8f72|sell|4kCUKMQJum19KFSdYUexVfsoHtb2kiWJ3b91FjALpump", ...

# run 2: new process, 1 iteration
start, cursor 2026-11-25T23:13:51.116Z, 45 keys remembered
poll 1
fetched 0, new 0, duplicates skipped 0, cursor 2026-11-25T23:13:51.116Z

# backoff, offline fake transport
error (copy.top answered 429: slow down); retry in 2s
error (copy.top answered 503: unavailable); retry in 4s
answered: 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z after waits [2.0, 4.0]
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Reading it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The first call returned exactly 30 trades, the size of the tape.&lt;/strong&gt; With no cursor, that is the feed's whole memory. If the tape had been busier, older trades would already have been gone before the script started. That is why the cadence matters: the cursor can only fetch what the feed still keeps.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The second call returned 15, all newer than the cursor.&lt;/strong&gt; None of them repeats a trade from the first call, and the first of them (&lt;code&gt;23:13:22&lt;/code&gt;) comes after the cursor of the first call (&lt;code&gt;23:13:13&lt;/code&gt;). Nothing was skipped that the feed still held, and nothing was counted twice.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The restarted process picked up the cursor and the 45 remembered keys from the file&lt;/strong&gt; and asked for trades after &lt;code&gt;23:13:51&lt;/code&gt;. It got none, so it did not replay the 45 trades it had already handled.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The backoff waited 2 seconds, then 4,&lt;/strong&gt; and then took the valid answer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One honest note: in this run the de-duplication step never had anything to drop (&lt;code&gt;duplicates skipped 0&lt;/code&gt; every time). The server accepted the cursor as an exclusive bound, so no trade came back twice. The key set is insurance for the case where that changes, or where a crash replays a batch, and I did not trigger either case here. I would rather say so than stage a duplicate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where to take it next
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pass &lt;code&gt;traders="handle1,handle2"&lt;/code&gt; in the &lt;code&gt;trades&lt;/code&gt; call to follow a short watchlist in a single request. The method in &lt;code&gt;copytop&lt;/code&gt; 0.1.0 has no &lt;code&gt;traders&lt;/code&gt; argument, so call &lt;code&gt;Client().get("trades", {"traders": [...], "since": cursor})&lt;/code&gt; instead.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Replace the print with a queue, a database insert or a webhook.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;min_usd&lt;/code&gt; to keep only the larger trades.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If you only want events and not the raw tape, &lt;code&gt;Client.watch_alerts()&lt;/code&gt; in the same package does this cursor loop for you on the alerts feed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What this does not tell you
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Two runs, three calls, a few minutes.&lt;/strong&gt; Rate and ordering behavior under heavy load was not tested.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No trade id.&lt;/strong&gt; The key is built from four fields. If the feed ever adds a real id, use it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tape size is a limit.&lt;/strong&gt; If your process is down for more than the time 30 trades take to arrive, the gap cannot be recovered with a cursor. A trade feed is not an archive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Third-party text.&lt;/strong&gt; Symbols and names in the feed are written by others. Print them, do not execute or follow anything inside them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Figures are data, not advice. Past performance does not predict results, and trading memecoins can lose money, including everything you put in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://copy.top/developers" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;copy.top developers&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://pypi.org/project/copytop/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;copytop on PyPI&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://copy.top/research" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;copy.top research&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Multi-AZ in one region protects against a single-zone failure at a moderate premium: cross-zone data transfer, spare capacity in each zone, and a database standby that can double the database line. Multi-region adds a second copy of the serving stack, cross-region replication, and cross-region data transfer, and how close it gets to double depends on whether the second region runs as a warm standby, at full capacity, or serves live traffic (active-active). In &lt;a href="https://controlplane.com/blog/post/cloud-outage-tracker?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Control Plane’s tracker of 83 major cloud provider outages&lt;/a&gt; across five providers from July 2020 to November 2026, only 3 stayed inside a single zone and 42 stayed inside a single region, so multi-AZ covers the cheapest failure class and a second region covers most of the rest. On Control Plane, a second region is one more location added to a Global Virtual Cloud (GVC), Control Plane’s named set of locations, with no change to the workload definition.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disclosure: Control Plane publishes this guide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Eight facts decide whether a second region is worth its cost.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What multi-AZ survives.&lt;/strong&gt; A single-zone failure. Only 3 of 83 tracked major outages stayed inside one zone.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What a second region adds.&lt;/strong&gt; Survival of region-scoped outages. 42 of 83 outages stayed inside one region, so a second region of the same provider could contain up to 45 of the 83.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What neither covers.&lt;/strong&gt; The other 38 outages, 12 multi-region and 26 global or edge-wide, spread beyond one region of the provider.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Costs that grow.&lt;/strong&gt; Compute for the serving tier, a database replica, cross-region data transfer, global load balancing and health checks, observability, engineering time, and failover testing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Warm standby vs active-active.&lt;/strong&gt; Warm standby keeps the second region small and scales it on failover. Active-active runs both regions at serving capacity, about 2x for the serving tier when each region must carry full load.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;When multi-AZ is enough.&lt;/strong&gt; Your RTO tolerates hours if a whole region fails, your commitments fit what one region’s provider SLA backs, and no contract or regulator requires a second geography.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;When to add a region.&lt;/strong&gt; One long regional outage costs more than a year of the second region, a contract requires geographic recovery, or customers on another continent need low latency.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Where Control Plane fits.&lt;/strong&gt; Control Plane lets you &lt;a href="https://controlplane.com/blog/post/run-app-across-multiple-regions?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;run an app across multiple regions&lt;/a&gt; from a single workload definition, with each location scaling on its own load.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How much does multi-region high availability cost compared with a single region?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A second region has no flat multiplier because each cost line grows at a different rate, depending on whether the region runs as a warm standby or active-active.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Cost line&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Single region multi-AZ&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Second region warm standby&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Second region active-active&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Compute for the serving tier&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1x plus headroom to absorb one lost zone, about 1.5x across three zones&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Small baseline that scales on failover&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;About 2x the single-region serving tier at full capacity&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Database (standby or replica)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One standby in another zone&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One cross-region replica, possibly a smaller instance&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One cross-region replica sized for full load&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Storage and backups&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1x with in-region replication&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A second copy of critical data and backups&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Full storage in both regions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Data transfer&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$0.01 per GB each direction across zones on AWS, $0.01 per GiB on Google Cloud, no charge on Azure&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Replication traffic between regions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Replication plus any cross-region calls between services&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Load balancing and DNS health checks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Regional load balancer&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;New line item: global routing and health checks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;New line item: global routing and health checks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Observability and logs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1x&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Per-region monitors and dashboards, plus logs from standby replicas&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Per-host metrics grow with the fleet; request logs stay near 1x because traffic is split&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Engineering and on-call time&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Baseline&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Runbooks and on-call coverage for a second region&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Two regions to debug, plus write-conflict handling if both accept writes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Testing (game days)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Zone failover tests&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Scheduled region failover tests, including the scale-up path&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Scheduled region failover tests under live load&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compute is the largest line. A warm standby region runs a fraction of the primary’s capacity, so compute grows but does not double. An active-active deployment needs enough capacity in each region to absorb the other region’s traffic during a failure, which approaches 2x for the serving tier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Databases add a second charge. &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Concepts.MultiAZSingleStandby.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;RDS Multi-AZ&lt;/a&gt; keeps a synchronous standby replica in a different Availability Zone, and in a single-standby deployment the standby cannot serve read traffic. A second region needs a cross-region replica with its own compute and storage. &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/aurora-global-database.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Aurora Global Database&lt;/a&gt; replication lag is typically under a second, and &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/pricing/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;you pay for replicated write I/O&lt;/a&gt; between the primary Region and each secondary Region, plus cross-Region data transfer. A &lt;a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/high-availability" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Cloud SQL for MySQL HA instance&lt;/a&gt; costs twice as much as a standalone instance for CPU, RAM, and storage, and that covers only the in-region standby. &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/reliability/reliability-sql-database" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Azure SQL geo-replication bills each secondary as a separate database&lt;/a&gt;, though a secondary used only for disaster recovery can be &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/active-geo-replication-overview?view=azuresql" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;designated standby to reduce licensing cost&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Data transfer adds a cross-region charge. &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/pricing/on-demand/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS charges $0.01 per GB in each direction&lt;/a&gt; between Availability Zones for EC2, RDS, ElastiCache, and network interfaces. &lt;a href="https://cloud.google.com/vpc/network-pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Google Cloud charges $0.01 per GiB between zones and $0.02 per GiB between regions&lt;/a&gt; within North America or within Europe. &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/reliability/availability-zones-overview" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Azure does not charge for data transfer between availability zones&lt;/a&gt; in the same region and &lt;a href="https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/pricing/details/bandwidth/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;charges $0.02 per GB outbound between regions&lt;/a&gt; within North America or within Europe, with inbound free.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Engineering time and testing are the costs that never appear on the invoice. Failover runbooks, game days, and an on-call rotation that covers two regions all take sustained work. The &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/design-guides/regions-availability-zones" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Azure Well-Architected guide&lt;/a&gt; rates cost as low for locally redundant or single-zone deployments, moderate for zone-redundant, and high for multi-region, because each region needs its own resources and cross-region data replication can add significant cost. Ways to trim these lines without losing the second region are in &lt;a href="https://controlplane.com/blog/post/cut-cloud-costs-keep-multi-region-redundancy?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;how to cut cloud costs while keeping multi-region redundancy&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What is the difference between multi-AZ and multi-region?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every major cloud draws the same two boundaries, zones inside a region and regions across geographies, and the outage data shows how much each boundary contains.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Design&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Survives&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Does not survive&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Outages contained (of 83)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Single zone&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Host and rack failures&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Zone, region, multi-region, and global outages&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Multi-AZ in one region&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A single-zone outage&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Region, multi-region, and global outages&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Second region, warm standby&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Zone and single-region outages, after the standby scales up&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Multi-region and global outages at the same provider&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Up to 45&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Second region, active-active&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Zone and single-region outages, with no scale-up step&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Multi-region and global outages at the same provider&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Up to 45&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Second provider&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Zone, region, multi-region, and global outages at one provider&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Failures in shared dependencies such as DNS or a CDN, unless those are also independent&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Up to 83, when DNS and edge paths are also independent&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On AWS, &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/using-regions-availability-zones.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Regions are separate geographic areas and Availability Zones are multiple, isolated locations within each Region&lt;/a&gt;. The &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/compute/sla/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;EC2 SLA&lt;/a&gt; commits to 99.99% monthly uptime at the Region level for instances running across two or more Availability Zones, and 99.5% for a single instance. &lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/aws-multi-region-fundamentals/aws-multi-region-fundamentals.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AWS Multi-Region Fundamentals&lt;/a&gt; says most AWS users can achieve their resilience objectives in a single Region by using multiple Availability Zones, and that multi-Region is warranted for the highest-tier workloads with bounded recovery time, data sovereignty, or latency to end users.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On Google Cloud, &lt;a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/regions-zones" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;regions are independent geographic areas that consist of zones&lt;/a&gt;. Spreading across zones reduces the risk of one infrastructure outage affecting all resources, and spreading across regions gives a higher degree of failure independence. Google’s deployment archetypes describe &lt;a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/deployment-archetypes/zonal" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;zonal deployments&lt;/a&gt; as a low-cost option for development and test environments and for applications that can tolerate downtime, &lt;a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/deployment-archetypes/regional" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;regional deployments&lt;/a&gt; as surviving zone outages while a region outage still takes them down, and &lt;a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/deployment-archetypes/multiregional" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;multi-regional deployments&lt;/a&gt; as protection against region outages with potentially higher cost and more operating complexity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On Azure, &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/reliability/availability-zones-overview" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;availability zones are separate groups of datacenters in a region with independent power, cooling, and networking&lt;/a&gt;, and they do not protect against a full-region outage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 38 outages that spread across several regions or were global or edge-wide need independence from the provider’s shared layer: a second provider, a second DNS path, or a second CDN. Controls for that scope are in &lt;a href="https://controlplane.com/blog/post/reduce-blast-radius-cloud-provider-outage?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;how to reduce the blast radius of a cloud provider outage&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Is multi-AZ enough for high availability?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Multi-AZ is enough when a full-region outage is a risk your business can absorb, and six signals show when it is not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Signal&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Multi-AZ is enough&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Add a second region&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Contract or SLA commitment&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Your commitment fits what one region’s provider SLA backs, with no geographic recovery clause&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You commit to more than what one region’s provider SLA backs, or a contract requires geographic recovery&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;RTO and RPO&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hours of recovery time are acceptable if a whole region fails&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Recovery in minutes during a regional outage&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Revenue lost per hour of downtime&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Moderate; the team can restore from cross-region backups&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;High enough that one long regional outage costs more than a year of the second region&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Regulatory or customer requirement&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No requirement for a second geography&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A regulator or customer contract requires recovery in a separate geography&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Customers in other geographies&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Users are concentrated on one continent&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A significant user base on another continent needs low latency&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Team size and on-call capacity&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A small team with no capacity to run two regions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A team that can staff on-call and keep runbooks current for two regions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Static stability matters in either design. Each zone in a multi-AZ deployment should already have enough provisioned capacity to absorb traffic from a failed zone without launching new instances, because scaling calls go through the provider’s control plane, which may be impaired during the incident. The same principle applies one level up, because a warm standby that must scale during a region outage depends on the secondary region’s control plane being healthy. To match the design to your recovery objectives, start from &lt;a href="https://controlplane.com/blog/post/multi-region-failover-rto-rpo-budget?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the RTO, RPO, and budget for multi-region failover&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What does a warm standby region cost compared with active-active?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Warm standby trades a cheaper second region for a scale-up step during failover, and active-active pays for full capacity to remove that step.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A warm standby region runs a reduced number of instances, often the minimum needed to accept traffic and start scaling. Its compute cost is a fraction of the primary region’s. The database replica runs continuously and incurs full replication charges, and it can use a smaller instance class if you accept slower queries until it is resized. The risk is that the scale-up depends on the secondary region’s control plane responding while a large event may be stressing shared provider infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An active-active deployment keeps full serving capacity in both regions, which doubles serving compute. Failover has no scale-up step, because the healthy region already carries traffic and has room for the rest. Replication charges are similar in both models because the database stays synchronized either way. Cross-region data transfer can be higher in active-active when a service in one region reads data or calls services in the other, for example when every write must reach a single primary database.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Testing costs more for active-active because you validate routing, data consistency, and write handling under live load. Warm standby testing is simpler but must still prove the scale-up path end to end. The two patterns are compared line by line in &lt;a href="https://controlplane.com/blog/post/active-active-vs-active-passive-cost?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;active-active vs active-passive cost&lt;/a&gt;, and the decision for a growing company is in &lt;a href="https://controlplane.com/blog/post/multi-region-active-active-worth-cost-growing-saas?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;whether multi-region active-active is worth the cost for a growing SaaS company&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How do you cut the cost of a second region?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Five practices lower the bill for a second region without giving up the protection it buys.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run the second region small and scale it on failover, and accept the risk that the scale-up depends on that region’s control plane. Warm standby is the cheapest pattern that keeps the serving tier running in a second region; pilot light, which replicates data but keeps compute off, costs less and recovers more slowly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Replicate only the data that needs cross-region protection. Classify tables and buckets by recovery priority and replicate accordingly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run stateless tiers in both regions and keep one write primary. Stateless services serve from either region without coordination, and a single primary avoids write-conflict handling.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use one configuration for both regions. Drift between regions causes failover failures and wastes engineering time, and one definition deployed to both regions removes that class of bugs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Test failover on a schedule. Regular game days catch drift and prove the standby works, and a scheduled test costs less than finding a broken failover during a real outage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How Control Plane fits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Control Plane handles Day 2 operations for you, including autoscaling, built-in observability, and security. One GVC can span regions on AWS, Google Cloud, Azure, and Oracle Cloud Infrastructure (OCI), plus Kubernetes clusters you register and your own hardware, and every workload in the GVC runs in each of its locations from one workload definition, so a second region is a location added to the GVC. Adding a location provisions it for every workload, and removing one drains its replicas and shifts traffic to the locations that remain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By default, DNS sends clients to the nearest healthy location. Control Plane’s location routing options add a priority tier per location: within the highest-priority group the lowest-latency location is chosen, and if every location in that group is unavailable the next group is used. For a primary with a standby, put the primary in a higher-priority tier and the standby in a lower one. Autoscaling sets min and max replicas per workload, with per-location overrides, and each location scales on its own load, so the standby location can run fewer replicas until it takes traffic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You pay for the CPU and memory a workload reserves, per location, with no contracts or minimums (&lt;a href="https://controlplane.com/pricing?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;pricing&lt;/a&gt;). Control Plane offers a 99.999% SLA for workloads that run at least two replicas in at least two locations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the database tier, Control Plane’s &lt;a href="https://docs.controlplane.com/template-catalog/overview?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Template Catalog&lt;/a&gt;, its library of ready-to-install apps, includes a &lt;a href="https://docs.controlplane.com/template-catalog/templates/postgres-multi-location?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Postgres Multi-Location template&lt;/a&gt; that stretches one cluster across a GVC’s locations in different regions and clouds, and automatically promotes a replica in a surviving location when the GVC has three or more locations. A &lt;a href="https://docs.controlplane.com/template-catalog/templates/pgedge?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;pgEdge template&lt;/a&gt; runs active-active Postgres with multi-master replication when both regions must accept writes. Control Plane provisions each location, routes traffic across locations, and autoscales replicas in each one, so the per-region configuration that makes a second region expensive to operate becomes an edit to the GVC’s location list.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These answers cover the cost, coverage, and timing questions teams ask before adding a second region.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How much does multi-region high availability cost compared with a single region?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A warm standby region adds a fraction of the primary’s compute plus cross-region replication and data transfer, while active-active approaches 2x for the serving tier; database replicas, global routing, observability, and engineering time are additional lines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is multi-AZ enough for high availability?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes, for most SaaS workloads whose RTO can absorb a full-region outage and that have no contract requiring a second geography. AWS says most users can meet their resilience objectives in one Region with multiple Availability Zones, and Control Plane’s tracker logs only major outages, so routine zone failures, which multi-AZ absorbs, are undercounted there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Does multi-region double my cloud bill?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not necessarily. Warm standby keeps the total well under 2x because the second region runs small. Active-active doubles serving compute, while some lines, such as engineering time, grow by less and cross-region data transfer is a new variable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What is the cheapest way to survive a region outage?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cross-region backups with a tested restore are the cheapest way to recover, with an RTO measured in hours. The cheapest way to keep serving is a warm standby with a small compute footprint and asynchronous database replication, which scales up on failover. A pilot light, which replicates data with compute off, costs less and recovers more slowly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How much does cross-region data transfer cost?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Between regions within North America or within Europe, Google Cloud charges $0.02 per GiB and Azure charges $0.02 per GB outbound, with inbound free on Azure. Between zones, AWS charges $0.01 per GB in each direction, Google Cloud $0.01 per GiB, and Azure nothing; check your provider’s page for each region pair. AWS also bills inter-Region transfer per GB; read the rate for your Region pair on the &lt;a href="https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/pricing/on-demand/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;EC2 pricing page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is active-active more expensive than active-passive?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Usually. Active-active pays for full capacity in both regions, while active-passive run as a warm standby keeps the second region small; a hot standby at full capacity costs about the same compute as active-active. Both pay similar database replication charges, and active-active can add cross-region calls between services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


When should a SaaS company add a second region?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When one long regional outage would cost more than a year of the second region, when a customer contract or regulation requires recovery in another geography, or when users on another continent need low latency. The &lt;a href="https://controlplane.com/blog/post/multi-region-failover-rto-rpo-budget?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;multi-region failover RTO, RPO, and budget guide&lt;/a&gt; turns those inputs into a design.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How do I run multiple regions without a platform team?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use Control Plane and define each workload once to run it in every location of a &lt;a href="https://docs.controlplane.com/concepts/gvc?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Global Virtual Cloud&lt;/a&gt;, Control Plane’s named set of locations. Traffic goes to the nearest healthy location, and Control Plane’s location routing options keep a lower-priority standby in reserve until the higher-priority locations are unavailable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What share of cloud outages does multi-AZ cover?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Of 83 major outages tracked across five providers from July 2020 to November 2026, 3 stayed inside a single zone, so multi-AZ would have contained those 3. A second region of the same provider could have contained up to 45, and the remaining 38 spread beyond one region.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Do I need a second cloud provider for resilience?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Only for the outages a second region cannot contain. 38 of 83 outages spread beyond one region or were global or edge-wide, and surviving those requires separate DNS, CDN, or provider paths. A Control Plane GVC can include locations on more than one cloud, so a second provider is another location in the same GVC.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://controlplane.com/requestdemo?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Talk to the Control Plane team&lt;/a&gt; about running a second region as one more location, or &lt;a href="https://console.cpln.io/signup" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;sign up free&lt;/a&gt; and deploy a workload to two locations.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on the &lt;a href="https://controlplane.com/blog/post/multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=syndication&amp;amp;utm_campaign=multi-az-vs-multi-region-cost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Control Plane blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Giving an AI agent an email address looks simple on paper. Spin up an SMTP relay, hook up an inbound webhook, hand the agent an API key, done.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In practice, the plumbing takes an afternoon. Keeping the domain from getting blacklisted takes most of the real engineering.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most builders hit the same three bad options first:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Give the agent your personal Gmail. Now a prompt injection or a stray tool call has access to your password resets, billing, and identity.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Buy a fresh domain per agent. Now you are managing DNS, SPF, DKIM, and cold-start warmup every time you spin up a new worker.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use a disposable burner service. Almost every service that matters (GitHub, Linear, Slack, Stripe) already blocklists burner domains, so OTPs never arrive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A shared domain with managed reputation solves this for developers, because one API call gives an agent a working address with zero DNS setup. The catch is that the moment your shared domain has decent deliverability, spammers and burner-account farmers show up immediately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The two abuse vectors that show up fast
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Running &lt;a href="https://theagentmail.net" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AgentMail&lt;/a&gt; (our MIT open-source agent email service on &lt;code&gt;@theagentmail.net&lt;/code&gt;) surfaced two distinct abuse patterns right away:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Outbound phishing and unsolicited cold blasts.&lt;/strong&gt; Someone creates a free trial account, names the org something generic, and immediately tries to blast cold outreach or phishing templates impersonating Meta or SaaS login alerts. If even a handful of those leave the server, spam complaints tank inbox placement for every legitimate agent on the domain.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Free burner OTP farming.&lt;/strong&gt; Automated scripts sign up for free trials just to receive signup and verification codes for other platforms, burning through addresses with zero intention of paying.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What actually stops it without breaking legit agents
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rate limits alone do not work, because ten phishing emails are enough to hurt domain reputation. Static keyword lists help for obvious templates, but spammers rephrase constantly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The setup that has worked well in production layers four checks:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Phone verification on free trials&lt;/strong&gt; so spinning up 50 throwaway orgs is not free.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Blocking inbound signup, OTP, and account-activation emails on free trials.&lt;/strong&gt; Free trials still get full normal send/receive and webhooks so developers can test their integration, while OTP/signup verification emails unlock as soon as an org adds a $5 prepaid deposit (which covers 5 mailbox-months at $1/mo and never expires).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A real-time AI outbound spam and phishing classifier&lt;/strong&gt; that inspects every outbound email before it leaves the server. Legitimate 1:1 agent correspondence, deploy alerts, and human handoffs pass right through; phishing, credential harvesting, and unsolicited mass spam get rejected synchronously with &lt;code&gt;400 SPAM_REJECTED&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cross-isolate locks and rate limits in Cloudflare Durable Objects&lt;/strong&gt; so concurrent requests cannot race trial creation or replay webhooks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Making it zero-friction for coding agents (Remote MCP + llms.txt)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The other lesson from watching developers integrate email into Claude Code, OpenCode, Cursor, and OpenClaw is that nobody wants to install another wrapper SDK or run a local stdio bridge just to check an inbox.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We just shipped a native Streamable HTTP remote MCP endpoint directly inside the Cloudflare Worker at &lt;code&gt;https://api.theagentmail.net/mcp&lt;/code&gt;. You can wire it into Claude Code with one command:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;claude mcp add --transport http agentmail https://api.theagentmail.net/mcp \
--header &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Authorization: Bearer am_..."&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Tokens are strictly scoped. An account-scoped key only exposes tools for that single mailbox (&lt;code&gt;get_mailbox&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;list_messages&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;get_message&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;send_email&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;get_attachment_url&lt;/code&gt;, and webhook management) and cannot list other mailboxes or touch org billing. If you prefer plain HTTP, pointing any coding agent at &lt;code&gt;https://theagentmail.net/llms.txt&lt;/code&gt; gives it the entire REST and MCP spec in one fetch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The whole stack (Cloudflare Workers, Durable Object guards, Resend, InstantDB, spam classifier, and Preact UI) is MIT open source on GitHub if you want to read the code or self-host it on your own domain: &lt;a href="https://github.com/uriva/agentmail" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/uriva/agentmail&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We make &lt;a href="https://ssrs2.net" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SSRS2&lt;/a&gt;, an engine that runs RDLC reports (the format of SQL Server Reporting Services and Report Viewer) on modern .NET, on Windows, Linux, macOS and in containers. Same reports, same code, PDF, Excel, Word and ten more formats, no GDI+.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It does one job: your existing reports keep working, wherever you deploy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then our customers started asking a different question.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


"Can we stop writing RDLC?"
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not "can you render it better". Can we stop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The reasons were always the same:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;New developers know HTML and CSS. Few of them want to learn a report designer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The design team already has the invoice. It is a web page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The app has a React dashboard, and someone wants it as a PDF by Friday.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But nobody can rewrite four hundred reports in a quarter. The reports that exist are tested, signed off, and somebody's month-end depends on them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the real request was: &lt;strong&gt;let us move to HTML, one report at a time, without a migration project.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why we didn't just say "use a browser"
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because that is what HTML to PDF means today. You start Chrome on a server that has no screen, through Puppeteer or Playwright, or through a library that hides a copy of Chromium in its package.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It works. It also means a browser in your deployment, a process to keep alive, and a sandbox to configure. Our customers came to SSRS2 to get rid of exactly that kind of dependency. Sending them back to it felt wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


So we built Weblitz
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://weblitzpdf.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Weblitz&lt;/a&gt; turns HTML, CSS and JavaScript into a PDF inside your own process. There is no browser inside. The layout, the fonts and the PDF writer are our own code; the page's scripts run in V8, so React, Vue and Chart.js pages build themselves before they print.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;WeblitzPdf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;HtmlToPdf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Render&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"&amp;lt;h1&amp;gt;Invoice 42&amp;lt;/h1&amp;gt;&amp;lt;p&amp;gt;...&amp;lt;/p&amp;gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;File&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;WriteAllBytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"invoice.pdf"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Pdf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The rule we set ourselves: &lt;strong&gt;it must print what Chrome prints.&lt;/strong&gt; We measure it against Chrome's own PDFs. On our 17 benchmark documents it scores 100% beside Chrome 156, box by box (19 November 2026). And on one-page documents it is up to 9× faster than Chrome driven by Puppeteer (Apple M4 Pro, 25 November 2026). Every measure, including the unflattering ones, is on the &lt;a href="https://weblitzpdf.com/proof" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;proof page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fn2e2zw5cyg3h7eh9l23i.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fn2e2zw5cyg3h7eh9l23i.png" alt="The first page of the same HTML report printed twice: by Chrome on the left, by Weblitz on the right. The two pages look the same." width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


One report at a time
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both engines sit in the same application, under the same licence key.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// The 400 reports you already have: untouched&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;report&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;LocalReport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;report&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;LoadReportDefinition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rdlcStream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;report&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DataSources&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Add&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReportDataSource&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Sales"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pdf&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;report&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Render&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"PDF"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// The new one, written as a web page&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;invoice&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;HtmlToPdf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Render&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Keep the month-end reports in RDLC for the next ten years if you like. Write the next one in HTML. Convert an old one when it needs a redesign anyway. There is no cut-over date, and no second invoice: one licence covers both.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Like its RDLC sibling, Weblitz also writes Excel, Word and PowerPoint files from the same page, with no Office on the machine. Those are first versions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F4fdsn86i3hld4x7xcuul.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F4fdsn86i3hld4x7xcuul.png" alt="Diagram of one application with two engines: the RDLC reports you have go through SSRS2, the next report, a web page, goes through Weblitz. One licence key, for .NET, Node.js, Python, Java and HTTP." width="800" height="375"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Not only .NET anymore
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A report engine that only speaks C# is its own kind of lock-in. With 2.0, both engines run from the languages reports are actually served from:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;SSRS2 (RDLC)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Weblitz (HTML)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;.NET&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;SSRS2.NETCore&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;WeblitzPdf&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Node.js&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;@ssrs2/ssrs2&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;weblitzpdf&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Python&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;ssrs2&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;weblitz&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Java&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;net.ssrs2:ssrs2&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;com.weblitzpdf:weblitzpdf&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Anything else&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;a Docker image that speaks HTTP&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;a Docker image that speaks HTTP&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each package carries the engine and the runtime it needs. There is no .NET to install on the Node, Python or Java side.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


No lock-in on the HTML side, on purpose
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the part we care about most.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An RDLC report is a file only a report engine can read. An HTML report is a web page. Open it in Chrome, press print, and you get the same pages Weblitz gives you. That is the whole point of matching Chrome.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So if you leave us one day, you keep every template. You swap one function call for Puppeteer and carry on, a little slower and with a browser to look after. We would rather keep you by being faster than by owning your file format.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What it does not do
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Weblitz prints pages; it does not click through them. A page that needs a login form filled or a button pressed is outside its scope. Tagged (accessible) PDFs are not there yet. The full list is on the &lt;a href="https://weblitzpdf.com/limitations" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;limitations page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both engines are at 2.0 release candidate, and 2.0 ships on 1 December. Without a licence key everything works, with a trial watermark on each page, and nothing to sign up for.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;dotnet add package WeblitzPdf --prerelease
npm install weblitzpdf
pip install --pre weblitz
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A licence is USD 799 a year per company: unlimited developers, servers and documents, for both engines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you have a page that prints differently from Chrome, &lt;a href="https://github.com/ssrs2-net/weblitz-url-to-pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;open an issue&lt;/a&gt; with its address. Those are the bug reports we like best.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;台灣的政府開放資料很多，但查一件生活上的事，通常要開四、五個網站：商品召回在經濟部標準檢驗局的頁面，藥品回收在食藥署的開放資料，消費爭議未出席名單在四個直轄市各自的資料平台，學區在六個教育局的 CSV 和 PDF 裡，補習班立案又回到教育部的另一個系統。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;我把這些公開資料整理成兩個可以搜尋的站：&lt;strong&gt;公開查&lt;/strong&gt;（商品安全、消費爭議、中古交易、補習班、團體訴訟）與 &lt;strong&gt;台灣學區查&lt;/strong&gt;（學區、幼兒園、補習班、課後照顧、高中職、大專、圖書館）。這篇說明每個模組收錄什麼、資料來自哪個資料集、有哪些處理上的坑，以及「查不到」代表什麼。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


公開查（gongkaicha.com）：九個查詢分頁加一個統編整合查詢
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;目前收錄 355,587 件，全部是政府公開資料的轉錄，不做評價、不做排名、沒有付費收錄。主要模組：&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;商品安全召回 6,427 筆&lt;/strong&gt;：標準檢驗局應施檢驗商品的召回訊息、食藥署邊境查驗不符合食品、交通部車輛安全資訊網的車輛召回改正。可用商品名稱、廠牌、型號、批號查，型號的連字號、空白、全形半形不影響比對。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;藥品・醫材回收 1,718 筆&lt;/strong&gt;（&lt;code&gt;data.gov.tw/dataset/6947&lt;/code&gt;）：回收分級、許可證字號、批號、原因；另有醫療器材安全警訊 1,000 件（&lt;code&gt;/14192&lt;/code&gt;）、廣告核准 14,208 筆（&lt;code&gt;/9826&lt;/code&gt;）、醫材說明書與包裝 39,782 張許可證（&lt;code&gt;/9578&lt;/code&gt;）。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;消費爭議無故未出席協商 4,126 筆&lt;/strong&gt;：臺北市、新北市、臺中市、臺南市依消費者保護自治條例公告的名單（分別來自 data.taipei、data.ntpc、opendata.taichung、data.tainan）。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;二手交易安心查&lt;/strong&gt;：NCC 電信設備型式認證 111,913 筆（&lt;code&gt;/5996&lt;/code&gt;）、內政部警政署 165 遭停止解析的涉詐網域 96,821 筆（&lt;code&gt;/176455&lt;/code&gt;）。買中古機、中古車之前先確認型號有沒有認證、對方網域有沒有被通報。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;補習班・課後照顧&lt;/strong&gt;：四都立案短期補習班 9,896 筆，加上內政部消防署消防安全檢查重大不合格場所 168 處（&lt;code&gt;/7069&lt;/code&gt;）。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;團體訴訟檔案&lt;/strong&gt;：投保中心進行中案件 63 件、賠償款尚未領取案件 103 件（&lt;code&gt;/11774&lt;/code&gt;）。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;不動產・建物&lt;/strong&gt;：不動產經紀業裁罰 1,793 筆（&lt;code&gt;/125419&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/134932&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/83769&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/160620&lt;/code&gt;）、政府採購法停權廠商 1,555 件（&lt;code&gt;/5988&lt;/code&gt;）、臺北市列管海砂屋與地震黃單建物、輻射屋清冊、土壤液化潛勢圖資。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;勞動・環保公告&lt;/strong&gt;：違反勞動法令事業單位 64,919 筆（&lt;code&gt;/109896&lt;/code&gt;）、職安法公告 632 筆（&lt;code&gt;/139300&lt;/code&gt;）、環保標章違規 117 筆（&lt;code&gt;/28236&lt;/code&gt;）。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;統編整合查詢&lt;/strong&gt;：輸入 8 位統一編號，把同一家公司的裁罰、停權、勞動、消費爭議公告放在同一頁。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;網址：&lt;a href="https://gongkaicha.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://gongkaicha.com/&lt;/a&gt;（各分頁 &lt;code&gt;/recall&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/drug-recall&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/dispute&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/used&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/cram&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/classaction&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/estate&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/labor&lt;/code&gt;）&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


台灣學區查（tweduinfo.com）：從里鄰學區到圖書館
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;目前收錄 22 個縣市、1,747 所國小・國中、11,088 筆里・鄰學區（6 個直轄市）、7,709 間立案幼兒園、17,961 間立案補習班、506 所高中職、139 所大專校院、616 間公共圖書館、395 間立案課後照顧中心（3 個縣市）。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;學區資料來自臺北、新北、桃園、臺中、臺南、高雄六個教育局的公告，其中臺南、高雄是 PDF 一覽表，逐表解析後把里・村・鄰的單位正規化。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;幼兒園來自教育部全國教保資訊網與統計處名錄（&lt;code&gt;/6086&lt;/code&gt;），補習班來自短期補習班資訊管理系統（&lt;code&gt;/16461&lt;/code&gt;），高中職與大專來自統計處名錄（&lt;code&gt;/6089&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/6091&lt;/code&gt;），圖書館來自國立公共資訊圖書館（&lt;code&gt;/99567&lt;/code&gt;）。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;提供免費、免金鑰的 JSON API：&lt;code&gt;GET /api/search?q=龍安&amp;amp;city=tpe&amp;amp;level=elem&amp;amp;per=20&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;GET /api/school/{id}&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;GET /api/stats&lt;/code&gt;。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;網址：&lt;a href="https://tweduinfo.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://tweduinfo.com/&lt;/a&gt;（&lt;code&gt;/preschool&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/cram&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/afterschool&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/highschool&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/university&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/library&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;/city/tpe&lt;/code&gt; 等）&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


做這兩個站時遇到的六個坑
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;同一份資料多種寫法&lt;/strong&gt;。藥品回收分級在原始資料裡有 &lt;code&gt;1/2/3&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;第一級&lt;/code&gt;、&lt;code&gt;二&lt;/code&gt; 混用。我的處理是統一成三級加「未標示」，並把原值保留在「原文」欄位，不自行判斷分級。轉錄的人一旦開始「判斷」，錯的就是自己而不是公告。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;官方主機對海外 IP 封鎖&lt;/strong&gt;。教育部短期補習班名冊主機、全國課後照顧中心全國檔都只允許境內連線。做法是改收錄縣市政府自己公開的名冊，並在關於頁標明「已收錄 3 個縣市」——&lt;strong&gt;未收錄不代表未立案&lt;/strong&gt;，這句話必須寫在畫面上，而不是只寫在心裡。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;只有 PDF 的資料&lt;/strong&gt;。學區一覽表、海砂屋列管清冊、輻射屋清冊都是 PDF。逐表解析時要保留「原文沒有的資訊不補寫、不推測」，門牌只到「號」，不含樓層與戶別。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;個資&lt;/strong&gt;。負責人、園長、班主任、教師姓名、個人手機、個人電子郵件一律不取得、不保存、不顯示，並用程式每日／每月自動檢查資料庫；公告上的個人姓名在頁面上遮罩。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;更新不能把資料洗掉&lt;/strong&gt;。每天抓一次，但遇到 0 筆或件數驟減超過 60% 時不覆蓋，保留前一日資料並記錄異常——來源網站掛掉一次，不該讓整個站變成空白。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;授權與標示&lt;/strong&gt;。各來源依「政府資料開放授權條款第 1 版」公開，兩個站都依該條款利用，每一頁標示來源機關、資料集編號與資料確認時間（公開查目前是 2026-11-26 05:46）。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


使用時請記住四件事
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;查到不等於違法&lt;/strong&gt;。消費爭議未出席是程序事實，不是違法認定；裁罰公告也可能在訴願或行政訴訟中。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;查不到不等於安全&lt;/strong&gt;。公告有時間差、有收錄範圍，資料集本身也可能只開放一部分（例如醫材警訊官方匯出上限 1,000 筆）。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;一切以官方公告為準&lt;/strong&gt;。本站是轉錄，入學、轉學、總量管制、招生名額、收費，都要回到各校與主管機關的公告。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;這兩個站不是政府網站&lt;/strong&gt;，沒有排名、沒有付費收錄、沒有廣告排序。&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;我是這兩個站的開發者（筆名 食前麵包）。兩個站都免費、免註冊，資料每天自動更新，更正或刪除請求請寄 &lt;a href="mailto:gjvndtjs88@gmail.com"&gt;gjvndtjs88@gmail.com&lt;/a&gt;。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;公開查：&lt;a href="https://gongkaicha.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://gongkaicha.com/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;台灣學區查：&lt;a href="https://tweduinfo.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://tweduinfo.com/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;如果你也在做開放資料的整理站，歡迎交流：怎麼處理 PDF、怎麼設計「查不到」的說明，往往比爬資料本身更花時間。&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://www.panda-security.xyz/blog/biswap-v3-burn-phantom-liquidity-exploit/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Biswap V3 Exploit: Burn Bug Drains 50,554 BUSD&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On &lt;strong&gt;24 November 2026 at 20:24:58 UTC&lt;/strong&gt;, a helper contract on &lt;strong&gt;BNB Smart Chain&lt;/strong&gt; interacted with a legacy &lt;strong&gt;BiswapPoolV3&lt;/strong&gt; WBNB/BUSD pool and sent &lt;strong&gt;50,553.922841094381842 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt; to an externally owned account in the same transaction. &lt;strong&gt;TenArmor Alert&lt;/strong&gt; flagged that transaction at &lt;strong&gt;01:31:48 UTC on 25 November&lt;/strong&gt; and estimated about &lt;strong&gt;$50,600&lt;/strong&gt; in losses. &lt;strong&gt;SlowMist&lt;/strong&gt; published a root-cause note at &lt;strong&gt;02:23:27 UTC&lt;/strong&gt;, putting the loss at about &lt;strong&gt;$51,000&lt;/strong&gt; and attributing it to a liquidity-accounting flaw in the pool's &lt;code&gt;burn()&lt;/code&gt; function. &lt;strong&gt;Defimon Alerts&lt;/strong&gt; independently put the attacker's take at about &lt;strong&gt;50,553 BUSD (~$50.4K)&lt;/strong&gt; and dated the incident to 24 November.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Independently, we read the same BNB Chain transaction and the pool's token balances. The call succeeded in &lt;strong&gt;block 135526420&lt;/strong&gt;. The pool's BUSD balance fell from &lt;strong&gt;51,075.480075210664799733&lt;/strong&gt; to &lt;strong&gt;81.501165597941276329&lt;/strong&gt;, a drop of &lt;strong&gt;50,993.978909612723523404 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt;. Its WBNB balance fell by &lt;strong&gt;0.327164510994583018 WBNB&lt;/strong&gt;. The attacker address received &lt;strong&gt;50,553.922841094381842 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt; and no WBNB. That BUSD amount is the token quantity itself; at a one-to-one peg it is about &lt;strong&gt;$50,554&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a defensive account of a public incident, not a reconstruction of how to repeat it. Biswap had not published an official loss figure or a public statement at the time of writing, and we have not seen confirmation that the affected pool — or other pools that share the same code — has been paused or patched.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Happened
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Biswap is a DEX on BNB Smart Chain. Public write-ups describe Biswap V3 as an &lt;strong&gt;iZiSwap-style&lt;/strong&gt; concentrated-liquidity design, with liquidity placed between ticks and swaps executed through dedicated modules. The victim is the &lt;strong&gt;WBNB/BUSD&lt;/strong&gt; pool at &lt;code&gt;0xc83f96f87f586a858a14c89e16434460bac0b643&lt;/code&gt;, which TenArmor, SlowMist and Defimon all labeled as an older or legacy BiswapPoolV3 contract. The BUSD in the pool is the legacy BUSD token at &lt;code&gt;0xe9e7CEA3DedcA5984780Bafc599bD69ADd087D56&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That "legacy" label matters for scope: the confirmed drain is this contract, not every Biswap market and not every iZiSwap-style pool. We have not confirmed additional exploited pools, and unconfirmed code exposure is not the same thing as a confirmed loss.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The transaction all three alert desks cited is &lt;a href="https://bscscan.com/tx/0x13b8310513cc7ff91ad24a966f6ced99aa535d2aef8a231e38737401c8e7f78c" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;&lt;code&gt;0x13b83105…e7f78c&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. It was sent by &lt;code&gt;0x1ec9d79d1a7c47c3887d8fc322c88e56b16e90a5&lt;/code&gt; to a helper at &lt;code&gt;0x6161a9ca16f213c3373c07bcc0c4b4a4705340be&lt;/code&gt; and succeeded at &lt;strong&gt;20:24:58 UTC&lt;/strong&gt;. TenArmor's alert preview on BscScan records the same timestamp.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the Transaction Moved
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The receipt shows a clear economic path. We describe the verified transfers, not a method for repeating them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The helper first borrowed &lt;strong&gt;11,283 WBNB&lt;/strong&gt; from the PancakeSwap V3 USDT/WBNB pool at &lt;code&gt;0x172fcd41e0913e95784454622d1c3724f546f849&lt;/code&gt;, the 0.01% fee tier. After a series of interactions with the victim pool, it received &lt;strong&gt;11,283.277957284066035619 WBNB&lt;/strong&gt; from that pool and paid only &lt;strong&gt;63,263 wei of BUSD&lt;/strong&gt; back in — about &lt;strong&gt;6.3 × 10⁻¹⁴&lt;/strong&gt; BUSD. Defimon attributed that exchange to a &lt;code&gt;swapY2XDesireX&lt;/code&gt; call. Those two transfer amounts match the receipt exactly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The helper then repaid &lt;strong&gt;11,284.1283 WBNB&lt;/strong&gt; to the PancakeSwap V3 pool. The increment is exactly &lt;strong&gt;1.1283 WBNB&lt;/strong&gt;, a &lt;strong&gt;0.01%&lt;/strong&gt; fee on the 11,283 WBNB loan. That fee was funded from two sources in the same receipt: &lt;strong&gt;0.327164510994583018 WBNB&lt;/strong&gt; taken from the victim pool, which is the amount the pool's WBNB balance fell, and &lt;strong&gt;0.801135489005416982 WBNB&lt;/strong&gt; bought on the PancakeSwap V2 WBNB/BUSD pair at &lt;code&gt;0x58f876857a02d6762e0101bb5c46a8c1ed44dc16&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;strong&gt;440.056068518341681404 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt;. Those two WBNB amounts add up to the flash-loan fee exactly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Finally, the helper sent &lt;strong&gt;50,553.922841094381842 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt; to the attacker EOA. Immediately before the transaction, both the helper and the attacker held zero BUSD and zero WBNB. Afterwards the attacker held that BUSD amount and the helper was empty again. The &lt;strong&gt;50,993.98 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt; that left the victim pool is larger than the attacker's &lt;strong&gt;50,553.92 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt; payout by the &lt;strong&gt;440.06 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt; spent on the fee, so the two figures reconcile. A flash loan only rents size for one transaction; the unusual part is that the Biswap pool paid more than &lt;strong&gt;11,283 WBNB&lt;/strong&gt; for essentially no BUSD. Our &lt;a href="https://www.panda-security.xyz/blog/flash-loan-attack/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;flash loan attack&lt;/a&gt; overview covers that funding pattern in general terms.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Burn Accounting Flaw
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SlowMist's disclosure is the most specific public account of the bug, and Defimon's description of the later swap is consistent with it. According to SlowMist, &lt;code&gt;burn()&lt;/code&gt; failed to decrement the pool's &lt;strong&gt;active liquidity&lt;/strong&gt; when both withdrawal components calculated at the current price point were zero, including when a very small Y-token amount &lt;strong&gt;rounded down to zero&lt;/strong&gt;. In that state, a position could be economically removed while the pool continued to treat part of its liquidity as still active. SlowMist called the leftover figure &lt;strong&gt;phantom liquidity&lt;/strong&gt;. A later swap could then trade against liquidity that was no longer backed by the corresponding reserves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is an accounting failure in position removal, not a broken oracle and not a failure of BNB Chain itself. The profitable step was a mispriced swap, but the swap was mispriced because the pool's active-liquidity accounting had already diverged from its token balances.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We are not publishing a reproduction. The snippet below is a simplified illustration of the skipped decrement SlowMist described, not the pool's production code and not a working procedure:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;// Simplified illustration of the reported skip, not Biswap's source.
function burn(/* position and amount args */) external {
(uint256 amountX, uint256 amountY) = amountsAtCurrentPoint(/* ... */);
// The position can still be updated and any tokens owed can still be paid.
// Reported bug: when both components are zero, including a Y amount
// that rounded down to zero, active liquidity is left unchanged.
if (amountX != 0 || amountY != 0) {
activeLiquidity -= liquidityToRemove;
}
}
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Integer rounding in concentrated-liquidity math is routine. A tiny position can produce an amount that is positive in real numbers and zero after truncation. If "both components are zero" is treated as a reason to skip the liquidity decrement, the tokens backing the position can leave while the pool still believes the liquidity is there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the same broad class of failure as other reserve or share-accounting bugs. In the &lt;a href="https://www.panda-security.xyz/blog/tendril-sell-hook-burn-sync-usdt-drain/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TENDRIL incident&lt;/a&gt;, a sell hook changed a pair's reserves before the swap had finished. Here, the distortion is inside the pool's own active-liquidity variable. In both cases, a swap that looks ordinary at the ABI level is economically unsafe because internal accounting no longer matches assets on hand.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Loss Figures
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Public numbers measure slightly different things, and they are consistent once that is taken into account:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Framing&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Figure&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Source&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Contemporaneous loss estimate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~$51,000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SlowMist TI Alert; also recorded by DefiLlama&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Early monitoring estimate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~$50,600&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;TenArmor Alert&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reported attacker take&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~50,553 BUSD (~$50.4K)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Defimon Alerts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;BUSD received by the attacker EOA&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;50,553.922841094381842 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;Transfer&lt;/code&gt; in the exploit transaction&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;BUSD leaving the victim pool&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;50,993.978909612723523404 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;pool balance before and after the same block&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;BUSD remaining in the pool afterwards&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;81.501165597941276329 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;pool balance at the post-transaction block&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;WBNB leaving the victim pool&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;0.327164510994583018 WBNB&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;pool balance before and after; used toward the flash-loan fee&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The attacker's &lt;strong&gt;50,553.92 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt; is the cleanest figure for what left as profit. The pool's &lt;strong&gt;50,993.98 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt; decline is the reserve drain, of which &lt;strong&gt;440.06 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt; was swapped for WBNB to complete the loan repayment. SlowMist's &lt;strong&gt;about $51,000&lt;/strong&gt; and TenArmor's &lt;strong&gt;about $50,600&lt;/strong&gt; are rounded dollar estimates of those token amounts, not a second, independent payout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Aftermath and Scope
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At the time of writing, we had not found a public statement from &lt;a href="https://x.com/Biswap_Dex" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Biswap&lt;/a&gt; confirming the loss, a pause, a patch, or a plan for affected liquidity providers. Some observers noted that other iZiSwap-style forks, or other legacy BiswapPoolV3 contracts, could share similar &lt;code&gt;burn()&lt;/code&gt; logic. That is a reasonable review prompt, not evidence that any other pool has been exploited.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After the transaction, the victim pool still held &lt;strong&gt;81.50 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt; and about &lt;strong&gt;0.01 WBNB&lt;/strong&gt;. Those leftovers are not a promise that remaining liquidity is safe, and whether the same path still works has not been publicly confirmed. If you provided liquidity to older Biswap V3 pools, follow updates only from Biswap's own channels and treat unsolicited "refund" messages as phishing until the project describes a process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Lessons for Builders and Auditors
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Decrement active liquidity whenever a position is removed, including when both token amounts at the current price are zero.&lt;/strong&gt; A zero payout is not a no-op. The position has still left the book, and the next swap will keep using whatever active-liquidity figure remains.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Treat rounding to zero as a first-class case.&lt;/strong&gt; Concentrated-liquidity formulas truncate. Tests should include burns in which one or both withdrawal components collapse to zero, then assert that active liquidity and reserves still back one another before any swap is allowed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Do not let a later swap be the first check of broken accounting.&lt;/strong&gt; Fork tests that only check "burn does not revert" will miss this class of bug. If internal liquidity can diverge from balances, a seemingly ordinary swap becomes the drain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bound damage even when pricing is wrong.&lt;/strong&gt; A per-transaction cap on reserve outflows would not fix the accounting, but it would have turned a near-emptying of this pool into a smaller loss.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Review legacy forks as if they were still in production.&lt;/strong&gt; A pool does not become safer because a newer factory exists. If the old contract still holds tokens, its &lt;code&gt;burn()&lt;/code&gt; and swap paths are still in scope. Our &lt;a href="https://www.panda-security.xyz/blog/smart-contract-security-checklist/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;smart contract security checklist&lt;/a&gt; is a practical place to park review items for accounting edges like this.&lt;/p&gt;

