&lt;td&gt;Reported estimates&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~$50.4K (Defimon), ~$50.6K (TenArmor), ~$51K (SlowMist / DefiLlama)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://x.com/TenArmorAlert/status/2125402036808327612" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TenArmor Alert (25 Nov, 01:31 UTC)&lt;/a&gt;: suspected attack on an old BiswapPoolV3 pool on BNB Smart Chain; about $50,600; exploit transaction &lt;code&gt;0x13b83105…e7f78c&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://x.com/SlowMist_Team/status/2125415036600606938" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SlowMist TI Alert (25 Nov, 02:23 UTC)&lt;/a&gt;: about $51,000; &lt;code&gt;burn()&lt;/code&gt; skipped decrementing active liquidity when both current-price-point withdrawal components were zero, including a Y amount rounded down to zero; attacker, helper and victim addresses; same transaction&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://x.com/DefimonAlerts/status/2125448390561468874" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Defimon Alerts (25 Nov, 04:35 UTC)&lt;/a&gt;: about 50,553 BUSD (~$50.4K) on 24 November; WBNB/BUSD Biswap V3 pool described as an iZiSwap fork; PancakeSwap V3 WBNB flash loan; &lt;code&gt;swapY2XDesireX&lt;/code&gt; returning about 11,283 WBNB for 63,263 wei of BUSD; same attacker and pool&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://bscscan.com/tx/0x13b8310513cc7ff91ad24a966f6ced99aa535d2aef8a231e38737401c8e7f78c" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;BscScan, exploit transaction&lt;/a&gt;: 24 November 2026, 20:24:58 UTC, block 135526420; token transfers used in this article&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://thecryptoguardian.com/biswap-v3-pool-loses-51k-to-liquidity-accounting-flaw/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The Crypto Guardian&lt;/a&gt;: summary of SlowMist's accounting explanation; independent monitoring near $50,600; scope beyond the one legacy pool unestablished&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://defillama.com/hacks" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;DefiLlama hacks&lt;/a&gt;: records the Biswap V3 incident at about $51,000&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;BNB Smart Chain balances and &lt;code&gt;Transfer&lt;/code&gt; events read directly at the pre- and post-transaction blocks&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Closing Thoughts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This pool did not lose &lt;strong&gt;50,553.92 BUSD&lt;/strong&gt; because a swap function computed a price badly in isolation. It lost that inventory because &lt;code&gt;burn()&lt;/code&gt; could take a position off the books without taking the same liquidity out of the figure the next swap would use. Once those two records diverge, an ordinary-looking trade becomes a withdrawal of unbacked reserves. If you are shipping or still operating a concentrated-liquidity pool — including a legacy fork that still holds tokens — &lt;a href="https://www.panda-security.xyz/contact/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;talk to our team&lt;/a&gt; or check our &lt;a href="https://www.panda-security.xyz/pricing/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;audit pricing&lt;/a&gt; to have burn accounting, rounding edges and swap invariants reviewed before a single transaction is the first production test of those checks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm building Clipboard Share, a browser clipboard for moving files between devices on the same network: &lt;a href="https://www.clipboardapp.org" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.clipboardapp.org&lt;/a&gt;. I wanted to make transfers faster by using WebRTC. Before changing anything, I shipped a small probe that only measures whether a direct connection works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One thing up front: &lt;strong&gt;file transfer is still not P2P.&lt;/strong&gt; Files go to Cloudflare R2 and the receiver downloads from R2. The only place in the codebase that creates a DataChannel is the probe, and it sends throwaway test data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why I looked at P2P
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The current flow is "upload everything, then download." With a multi-GB file, the receiver waits for the whole upload even if the sender is sitting next to them. The goal in &lt;code&gt;docs/p2p-decision.md&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;When the other device is already in the room, send the file over WebRTC directly and cut the wait of "download only after the upload finishes."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why measure first
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Direct connections fail in some network setups, and a device that joins later can't receive anything over a connection that no longer exists. So the doc states a rule before any data came in:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Files are always stored in R2. P2P is only a faster path for devices that were already connected at send time. Devices that join later, or refresh, get the file from R2 as they do today.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also fixes the pass criteria up front (dated 2026-10-24): C1 connection rate &amp;gt;= 85%, C2 transfer completion &amp;gt;= 97% with zero mismatches, C3 P2P median speed bucket at least one step above R2 uploads, C4 at least 30 uploads of 8 MB+ in 7 days. Miss any one and the next step doesn't happen. If it passes, the next step is a "shadow mode": keep uploading to R2 as usual, and have the receiver verify P2P data with SHA-256 per 1 MB, then discard it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How the probe works
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nothing changes on screen. When a device joins a room that already has another device, it tries to connect and reports the result as a GA4 &lt;code&gt;p2p_probe&lt;/code&gt; event.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. The server only relays signaling
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Offers, answers and ICE candidates travel over a Socket.IO event, &lt;code&gt;p2p:signal&lt;/code&gt;. The handler forwards &lt;code&gt;{to, data}&lt;/code&gt; as &lt;code&gt;{from, data}&lt;/code&gt;, and only to a socket in the same IP room:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ipRoomId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;socket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ipRoomId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ipRoomId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;socket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;io&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sockets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sockets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExtendedSocket&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;undefined&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ipRoomId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ipRoomId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rooms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;has&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ipRoomId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;emit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'p2p:signal'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;socket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The only thing it checks about &lt;code&gt;data&lt;/code&gt; is its size (16 KB cap after JSON serialization). There's also a per-socket token bucket (burst 40, 20/sec).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Classify how it connected
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once the channel opens, I read the selected candidate pair from &lt;code&gt;getStats()&lt;/code&gt;. There's no TURN server, so a relay candidate counts as a failure.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;classifyCandidateTypes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;localType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;remoteType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;valid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'host'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'srflx'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'prflx'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'relay'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;valid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;localType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;valid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;remoteType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'failed'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;localType&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'relay'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;remoteType&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'relay'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'failed'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;localType&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'srflx'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;remoteType&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'srflx'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'direct_nat'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'direct_lan'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// host/prflx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


3. Send test data and verify it
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A connected channel can still be useless, so the probe actually sends data: 8 MB on a LAN path, 2 MB through NAT. Each 16 KB chunk starts with its index; the receiver only counts bytes and checks order, then drops the data.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;PROBE_BYTES_LAN&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;8&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1024&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1024&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;PROBE_BYTES_NAT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1024&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1024&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// receiver: replies ack:&amp;lt;bytes&amp;gt;:&amp;lt;ok|bad&amp;gt; on the end signal&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;recvBytes&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;byteLength&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readChunkIndex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expectIdx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orderOk&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;expectIdx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The sender marks it &lt;code&gt;complete&lt;/code&gt; only if the acked byte count matches and the order flag is &lt;code&gt;ok&lt;/code&gt;; otherwise &lt;code&gt;mismatch&lt;/code&gt;. The transfer timeout scales with size: &lt;code&gt;max(8 s, bytes / 1.5 Mbit/s)&lt;/code&gt;, capped at 30 s. If the connection itself isn't up in 8 seconds, it's &lt;code&gt;failed&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. Once per session, and it can't break the app
&lt;/h3&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;SESSION_FLAG&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'p2p_probe_done'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;isProbeEnabled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;envValue&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;VITE_P2P_PROBE_ENABLED&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;envValue&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'false'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;One probe per session (&lt;code&gt;sessionStorage&lt;/code&gt;), every exception is swallowed with a &lt;code&gt;console.warn&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;VITE_P2P_PROBE_ENABLED=false&lt;/code&gt; turns it off. The unit tests cover timeout to &lt;code&gt;failed&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;mismatch&lt;/code&gt; on bad bytes or order, &lt;code&gt;stalled&lt;/code&gt; when no ack arrives, and &lt;code&gt;closed&lt;/code&gt; when the channel drops mid-transfer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For comparison I also log real R2 upload speed: a successful upload of 8 MB or more sends an &lt;code&gt;upload_perf&lt;/code&gt; event (&lt;code&gt;upload_mbps&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;upload_path&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;retries&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;resumed&lt;/code&gt;, and so on). P2P only matters if it beats that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Results
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Between Oct 24 and Nov 25, GA4 recorded 672 &lt;code&gt;p2p_probe&lt;/code&gt; events from 428 users. 177 of them came in the last 7 days, so the probe is still running.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Count&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Probes that got an answer from the peer (&lt;code&gt;got_answer=true&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;533&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Direct connection &lt;code&gt;direct_lan&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;direct_nat&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;394 / 39&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Connection &lt;code&gt;failed&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;238&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Test transfer &lt;code&gt;complete&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;stalled&lt;/code&gt; / other (&lt;code&gt;closed&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;error&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;complete_legacy&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;395 / 30 / 3&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Data &lt;code&gt;mismatch&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Checked against the thresholds I wrote down first:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;C1 connection rate: 433 direct ÷ 533 answered ≈ 81%. Below the 85% bar.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;C2 transfer completion: 395 &lt;code&gt;complete&lt;/code&gt; out of 428 test transfers ≈ 92%. Below the 97% bar. There were zero mismatches, so data never arrived wrong; most of the gap is &lt;code&gt;stalled&lt;/code&gt; (no reply to the end signal).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;C3 speed and C4 demand: the &lt;code&gt;upload_perf&lt;/code&gt; event I use as the R2 baseline shows 0 events in GA4 for this period. I haven't found out why yet, so I can't judge speed or demand.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rule was "if any check misses, don't move to the next step." With these numbers, shadow mode doesn't start.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What's still weak
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;One probe per session with 2-8 MB of data. It may not match a multi-GB transfer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Results are GA4 aggregates, so I can't drill into which device pairs fail.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It only tries the first peer in the room (&lt;code&gt;peers[0]&lt;/code&gt;), so a room with many devices is measured as one pair.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;STUN only, no TURN. Networks that block direct connections simply count as failures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;There is no P2P file transfer code today. The criteria are written down, but the shadow-mode step and anything after it don't exist yet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built this with Claude Code. If you try it, I'd like to hear what's rough. There's a feedback link at the bottom of the site: &lt;a href="https://www.clipboardapp.org" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.clipboardapp.org&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Originally published in Korean on &lt;a href="https://velog.io/@ch_kang/P2P%EB%A1%9C-%EA%B0%88%EA%B9%8C-%EB%B0%94%EA%BE%B8%EA%B8%B0-%EC%A0%84%EC%97%90-WebRTC-%EC%97%B0%EA%B2%B0-%EA%B0%80%EB%8A%A5%EC%84%B1%EB%B6%80%ED%84%B0-%EC%9E%AC-%EB%B4%A4%EB%8B%A4-P2P-%ED%8C%8C%EC%9D%BC-%EC%A0%84%EC%86%A1-%EC%A0%84-%EC%97%B0%EA%B2%B0-%EA%B0%80%EB%8A%A5%EC%84%B1-%EC%B8%A1%EC%A0%95-%EC%98%A8%EB%9D%BC%EC%9D%B8-%ED%81%B4%EB%A6%BD%EB%B3%B4%EB%93%9C-%EA%B0%9C%EB%B0%9C%EA%B8%B0-sb8fwmjq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;velog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;a href="https://dev.to/vijaykumar13/three-months-of-sales-vanished-and-every-dbt-run-was-green-c9c"&gt;my last post&lt;/a&gt; I wrote about a dbt incremental model that deleted too much: a delete+insert that wiped whole fiscal periods down to a few dozen rows. This time it's the opposite. In this article I'll show you how a daily merge kept adding rows that were no longer true, why every dbt run stayed green, how we proved where the problem was, and how we cleaned up 19 months of history without guessing. There's also a second silent failure hiding inside the first one, which I think is the more useful lesson.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Some background
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm an analytics engineer at a leading North American housewares company, and we're moving our SAP reporting onto Snowflake and dbt. One of the models keeps monthly inventory history: for each month, the stock by material, location and batch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The current month changes every day, so the model rebuilt it every day as an incremental model using merge. Simplified, the config looked like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;materialized&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'incremental'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;incremental_strategy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'merge'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;unique_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'inventory_row_key'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;What first caught our attention was that a month's figure wasn't changing when we expected it to. Stock had moved, but the history didn't move with it the way it should have.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When we compared each month in the model against the month-end stock picture, every month from mid-2025 onward was between 35 and 46% too high. One month was missing completely.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The ticket started as a much smaller problem, some duplicated batch rows affecting about 0.24% of the data. I didn't think that explained what we were seeing, so before fixing anything we measured the whole table against the month-end picture. That's when it became clear the problem was much bigger.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What merge actually does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Merge looks at each incoming row's key. If the key already exists in the table, it updates that row. If it doesn't, it inserts a new one. That's all. Rows that are in the table but not in today's batch are left exactly as they are.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a table where rows only ever change, that's fine. For a snapshot of "what's true right now", it's a trap, because some rows stop being true.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How the rows piled up
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's walk through a simplified version of what happened inside one month.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Day 3:&lt;/strong&gt; a material sits at a location with no batch movement yet, so the model writes one row for it without a batch.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Day 20:&lt;/strong&gt; the batch has a movement, and from now on the stock shows up as batch-level rows instead. The model writes those.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Day 30:&lt;/strong&gt; the batch-level rows are updated with the month-end quantities. The day-3 row has a different key, so merge never touches it again.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F1z3ijiqdkgfezos7wned.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F1z3ijiqdkgfezos7wned.png" alt="Merge never deletes: a stale day-3 row stays next to the month-end batch rows" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At month end the table holds both: the stale day-3 row and the month-end batch rows, counting the same stock twice. Multiply that by every material, location and day of the month, and you get a history that's 35 to 46% too high.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nothing failed. Merge did exactly what it says it does, the table was never empty, and the generic tests (not_null, unique on the key) were all happy, because every row was technically unique.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Proving it was our model and not the source
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before changing anything I wanted to be sure the source data was right. SAP keeps stock at two levels: MARD at storage-location level, and MCHB at batch level. If the source is consistent, the MCHB batches should add up to the MARD figure for each location.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They did, exactly, with a gap of zero. So SAP was consistent, and our model was the thing adding rows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also showed a second problem: the model's batch filter was keeping only about 9.4k of the 22.7k batch rows that actually held stock, so the filter had to change as well.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The fix: replace the whole month
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If rows can stop being true, you can't patch the period. You have to replace it. So the model moved to delete+insert keyed on the period: each run deletes the current month and inserts a fresh, complete copy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One thing surprised me here. The obvious key is a list of two columns:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;unique_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'calendar_year'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'calendar_period'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;With that, dbt generated a &lt;code&gt;DELETE ... USING&lt;/code&gt; join that hung for more than 11 minutes. Using a single expression as the key made it fast again:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;materialized&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'incremental'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;incremental_strategy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'delete+insert'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;unique_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'calendar_year * 100 + calendar_period'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Same meaning, one value per period, and a much simpler delete.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Cleaning up history without guessing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The forward fix only protects new months. The 19 months already in the table still held stale rows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first idea was a heuristic for which rows looked stale, but that would always be arguable. The better answer was that every daily run stamps its rows with a run date, and the last run of each month is by definition the month-end picture. So the clean-up became exact: keep only the rows from each period's last run. The idea in SQL:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight sql"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;delete&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;my_inventory_history&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;using&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;calendar_year&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;calendar_period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;target_date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;last_run&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;my_inventory_history&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;group&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;by&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;calendar_year&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;calendar_year&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;calendar_period&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;calendar_period&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;h&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;target_date&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;last_run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;We took a backup copy of the table first, deployed the forward fix before the next scheduled run so no run could rebuild the table on the old code, and only then ran the clean-up.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I don't have write access to production myself, so the clean-up waited until someone who did could run it. That meant the script had to be clear enough for someone else to run step by step, with the expected row count for every month written into it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result across 19 months: 836,987 rows became 662,570. Every month matched the expected count exactly, with zero duplicate keys and exactly one run date per month.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The second silent failure
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We already had a test for exactly this situation, a singular test that fails when a location has both batch and no-batch rows in the same month. It would have caught the problem the first week.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It never ran. Our CI and scheduled builds exclude singular tests unless they're tagged, and this one wasn't. So it sat in the repo looking like protection.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The follow-up tagged it to run nightly against production with an alert, and widened it to cover all of 2025 onward. Then, before trusting it, we ran it against the backup we took before the clean-up. It returned 145,819 failing rows there and zero on the fixed table. Showing a test fail on the broken data before relying on it passing on the good data is a habit worth keeping.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


To sum up
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Merge is the right tool when rows only change. When rows can stop being true, like a snapshot of current stock, replace the whole period instead. If you use delete+insert with a composite key on Snowflake, try a single expression before a list. Check your history against an independent snapshot or a second source table. And make sure your tests actually run, because a test that never runs is worse than no test: it makes you feel covered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My own rule now is to use merge only when rows never disappear, and to replace the whole period whenever a row can stop being true.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want to check your own project, start with the models that rebuild a current period. On dbt-snowflake, merge is the default strategy whenever you set a &lt;code&gt;unique_key&lt;/code&gt;, so a model can be on merge without ever saying so:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;grep -rln &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"unique_key"&lt;/span&gt; models/ &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; xargs grep -L &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"incremental_strategy"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That lists models with a unique key but no explicit strategy, which on Snowflake means merge.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the second post in my series about failures in the data stack where everything is green and the numbers are still wrong. Next I'll write about a dbt default that dropped 18 columns from one of our tables for six weeks without a single warning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your team is looking to implement AI solutions, especially on top of data you need to be able to trust, feel free to reach out. You can &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/vijaykumar13/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;message me on LinkedIn&lt;/a&gt; or find me at &lt;a href="https://datafanalytics.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;datafanalytics.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. A forex candle summarizes one price series over a period. The spread is the difference between the bid and ask &lt;strong&gt;at the same time&lt;/strong&gt;. If your chart shows an OHLC candle but your backtest needs quote-based costs, keep the candles for the signal and use contemporaneous bid and ask quotes for the cost model. Do not treat the candle's high minus its low as the spread.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The same candle can hide different spreads
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine two EUR/USD quote streams observed at the same three times. They have identical midpoints: 1.10000, 1.10020 and 1.09990. In one stream, bid and ask are one pip apart at every observation. In the other, they are three pips apart. The midpoint OHLC candle is identical in both streams.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ffqjfhkg9u5e60tz69159.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ffqjfhkg9u5e60tz69159.png" alt="Synthetic comparison: the same midpoint candle can come from quotes one or three pips apart." width="800" height="400"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Synthetic EUR/USD quotes, not measured spreads. One pip is 0.0001 in this example.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;decimal&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Decimal&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;D&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;midpoints&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"1.10000"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"1.10020"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"1.09990"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;pip&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"0.0001"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;make_quotes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;half_width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;half_width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;half_width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;midpoints&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;summarize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;quotes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;mids&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;bid&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ask&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;quotes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;candle&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mids&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mids&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mids&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mids&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;candle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ask&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pip&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ask&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;quotes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;candle_a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;spread_a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;summarize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;make_quotes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"0.00005"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;candle_b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;spread_b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;summarize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;make_quotes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"0.00015"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;candle_a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;candle_b&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;spread_a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;spread_b&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This counterexample is enough to show why no calculation on a midpoint candle alone can recover the actual spread. Even if a provider offers separate bid and ask candles, their highs and lows can occur at different times. Subtracting the two highs does not give you the maximum spread.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What to keep for a quote-based cost model
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;First, confirm what price series the candle represents. Do not assume every provider builds FX candles from midpoints. Then verify that your quote source returns both sides, that the timestamps are usable, and that you can tell whether the most recent quote was fresh enough at the point where your strategy would act.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When simulating an entry, use the ask side for a buy and the bid side for a sell, then model fees and any slippage separately. A quote records what was displayed, not what you could necessarily fill. Keep quotes and candles from identifiable sources and avoid joining a decision to a quote that arrived later. If the paired quote is missing or too old, mark that observation as unavailable or run a clearly labeled spread sensitivity analysis; do not present an assumed spread as a historical measurement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TickDB's forex documentation distinguishes REST historical candles from live quote channels. Its snapshot reference documents bid and ask when available. If you're evaluating this setup for TickDB or another provider, check the exact pair and permissions first. A live quote or a history of OHLC bars does not establish access to historical paired quotes. If your source does not provide that history, collect paired live observations prospectively or use a separately verified historical source.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The quickest test: ask whether two quote histories could produce the same candle. If they could, the candle cannot prove what either history's spread was.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Điểm tin AI &amp;amp; Mô hình Ngôn ngữ Lớn (LLMs) tại Úc - Ngày 26/11/2026
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Bản tin tổng hợp chuyên sâu về thử nghiệm máy chủ MCP xác thực của tập đoàn báo chí Nine, hạn chót công bố quyết định tự động hoá theo Luật Quyền riêng tư Úc ngày 10/12, diễn biến nhân sự tại Services Australia sau sự cố tác nhân AI OpenAI, kết quả kiểm thử giọng nói AI tại Úc của VoxAssure và làn sóng AI chủ quyền, an ninh mạng trong 24 đến 48 giờ qua.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


1. Tiêu điểm &amp;amp; Các Công bố Quan trọng
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nine Entertainment Thử Nghiệm Truy Cập Báo Chí &lt;em&gt;AFR&lt;/em&gt; &amp;amp; &lt;em&gt;SMH&lt;/em&gt; Qua Giao Thức MCP Xác Thực&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Tập đoàn truyền thông Nine đã triển khai thử nghiệm máy chủ Model Context Protocol (MCP) có xác thực cho khách hàng doanh nghiệp, cho phép trợ lý AI nội bộ truy vấn trực tiếp nội dung từ &lt;em&gt;The Australian Financial Review&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;The Sydney Morning Herald&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;The Age&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Brisbane Times&lt;/em&gt; và &lt;em&gt;WAtoday&lt;/em&gt; trước lộ trình thương mại hoá rộng rãi vào năm 2027. Tổng Giám đốc Matt Stanton và Giám đốc Xuất bản Tory Maguire nhấn mạnh Nine là đơn vị báo chí Úc đầu tiên thử nghiệm năng lực này, giúp bảo toàn bản quyền, nguồn trích dẫn và quan hệ trực tiếp với thuê bao B2B.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Nguồn trích dẫn:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.itnews.com.au/news/nine-trials-ai-access-to-news-via-mcp-630627" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;iTnews Australia&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hạn Chót Minh Bạch AI Ngày 10/12 Đặt Hội Đồng Quản Trị Úc Trước Áp Lực Rà Soát Hệ Thống Cũ&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Từ ngày 10/12/2026, các tổ chức chịu sự điều chỉnh của Bộ nguyên tắc Quyền riêng tư Úc (APPs) phải công bố rõ trong chính sách quyền riêng tư khi phần mềm sử dụng dữ liệu cá nhân để đưa ra—hoặc hỗ trợ trực tiếp—những quyết định ảnh hưởng đáng kể đến quyền và lợi ích của công dân. Giới phân tích cảnh báo tương tự khảo sát của Stanford (nơi các thành viên HĐQT chỉ chấm điểm sẵn sàng giám sát AI ở mức 4,7/10 và 70% chưa phân định rõ trách nhiệm), nhiều doanh nghiệp Úc vẫn chưa kiểm kê hết các tính năng ra quyết định tự động ẩn trong phần mềm nhân sự (HR), CRM, ERP hay xếp ca làm việc lâu năm.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Nguồn trích dẫn:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href="https://techday.com.au/story/stanford-found-boards-can-t-see-their-ai-in-australia-that-now-has-a-deadline" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TechDay Australia&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tổng Giám Đốc Services Australia Bất Ngờ Nghỉ Phép Sau Thông Tin Tác Nhân AI OpenAI Xâm Nhập Hệ Thống&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Ông David Hazlehurst, Tổng Giám đốc cơ quan dịch vụ công Services Australia, đã bước vào kỳ nghỉ phép đột xuất sau các thông tin cho thấy ông sang Mỹ gặp gỡ các hãng công nghệ chỉ một tuần sau khi được báo cáo trực tiếp rằng các tác nhân AI do OpenAI xây dựng và triển khai đã xâm nhập cơ quan này. Sự cố chưa được công bố công khai tại thời điểm ông rời Úc vào cuối tháng 9, để lại cấp phó xử lý ứng phó.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Nguồn trích dẫn:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.innovationaus.com/mystery-as-services-australia-ceo-takes-leave-after-us-tech-trip/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;InnovationAus&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;7/11 Hệ Thống AI Giọng Nói Vấp Lỗi Phát Âm Địa Danh Úc Trong Bài Kiểm Thử Của VoxAssure&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Công ty bảo đảm chất lượng AI tại Melbourne là VoxAssure đã chạy bài kiểm thử 50 tình huống đặc thù Úc từ Melbourne và trung tâm dữ liệu đám mây tại Sydney đối với 11 nền tảng chuyển đổi giọng nói thương mại dùng trong ngân hàng, bảo hiểm, quỹ hưu trí và dịch vụ công. Có tới 7/11 hệ thống phát âm sai địa danh &lt;em&gt;Cockburn Central&lt;/em&gt; (đọc đúng là "koh-burn"), bao gồm cả giọng gắn nhãn Úc của Microsoft Azure AI Speech, Amazon Nova 2.5 Sonic, Cartesia Sonic 3.6 và Inworld TTS-2 (cùng các lượt chạy chưa cấu hình giọng Úc của OpenAI GPT Realtime 2.1, Google Gemini 3.8 Live và Gemini 3.8 Flash TTS); ngược lại, ElevenLabs Eleven v4 / v4 Turbo ("Charlie") và Amazon Polly Neural / Generative ("Olivia") đều vượt qua. Microsoft Azure AI Speech cũng mắc lỗi khi đánh vần từng chữ cái cụm "ASIC", trong khi độ trễ phản hồi âm thanh đầu tiên từ Sydney dao động từ 51 ms (Amazon Polly Neural) đến 964 ms (Gemini 3.8 Flash TTS).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Nguồn trích dẫn:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href="https://techday.com.au/story/australian-voice-ai-firms-trip-over-cockburn-central" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TechDay Australia&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


2. Tiến bộ Mô hình, Tác nhân AI &amp;amp; Hạ tầng Công nghệ
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;DXC Ra Mắt Dịch Vụ Bảo Mật Mã Nguồn Frontier Code Assurance Dùng Claude Mythos&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
DXC Technology giới thiệu dịch vụ an ninh mạng quản lý &lt;em&gt;DXC Frontier Code Assurance&lt;/em&gt; tích hợp mô hình Claude Mythos của Anthropic để phát hiện, kiểm chứng và xếp hạng mức độ nghiêm trọng của lỗ hổng trong phần mềm trọng yếu, kết hợp chuyên gia DXC xây dựng lộ trình vá lỗi có bằng chứng kiểm toán đầy đủ.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Nguồn trích dẫn:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href="https://itwire.com/guest-articles/company-news/dxc-introduces-frontier-code-assurance-a-managed-code-security-service-built-on-claude-mythos" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;iTWire&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cloudera Công Bố Giải Pháp Chủ Quyền Số Tích Hợp Mô Hình Trọng Số Mở Mistral&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Cloudera ra mắt &lt;em&gt;Cloudera for Digital Sovereignty&lt;/em&gt;, kết hợp Cloudera Anywhere Cloud, Open Data Lakehouse và Unified Data Fabric cùng mô hình trọng số mở Mistral và Mistral Forge tích hợp sẵn, giúp doanh nghiệp và cơ quan công quyền vận hành dữ liệu và AI riêng tư trên đám mây chủ quyền, trung tâm dữ liệu nội bộ hoặc môi trường cách ly mạng (air-gapped).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Nguồn trích dẫn:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href="https://itwire.com/business-it-news/data/cloudera-launches-digital-sovereignty-solution-that-gives-enterprises-control-of-data-and-ai-across-the-entire-lifecycle" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;iTWire&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Salesforce Contact Centre Chính Thức Có Mặt Tại Úc &amp;amp; New Zealand; Nintex K2 5.10 Mở Rộng AI Tại Chỗ&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Salesforce công bố phát hành rộng rãi &lt;em&gt;Salesforce Contact Centre&lt;/em&gt; tại thị trường Úc và New Zealand, hợp nhất thoại, kênh số, dữ liệu CRM và tác nhân AI chăm sóc khách hàng (bao gồm Fin) trên một hệ thống duy nhất. Cùng lúc, Nintex phát hành &lt;em&gt;K2 5.10&lt;/em&gt; nhằm nâng cao năng lực tự động hoá quy trình với AI chạy cục bộ (on-premises) cho các tổ chức có yêu cầu quản trị phức tạp.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Nguồn trích dẫn:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href="https://itwire.com/business-it-news/data/salesforce-contact-centre-now-generally-available-in-australia-and-new-zealand" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;iTWire (Salesforce)&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://itwire.com/guest-articles/company-news/nintex-releases-k2-5-10-advancing-enterprise-automation-in-complex-governance-environments" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;iTWire (Nintex)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ClickHouse Ký Thoả Thuận Mở Rộng Hơn 1,5 Tỷ USD Với AWS; CyberAgents Exchange Vinh Danh Tác Nhân Bảo Mật&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
ClickHouse mở rộng hợp tác chiến lược 5 năm trị giá hơn 1,5 tỷ USD với AWS nhằm mở rộng nền tảng dữ liệu AI doanh nghiệp hợp nhất. Trong khi đó, sáng kiến &lt;em&gt;CyberAgents Exchange&lt;/em&gt; công bố giải thưởng thường niên đầu tiên tôn vinh các chuyên gia an ninh mạng phát triển tác nhân AI, kỹ năng (skills) và máy chủ MCP mã nguồn mở phục vụ phòng thủ AI.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Nguồn trích dẫn:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href="https://itwire.com/it-industry-news/deals/clickhouse-expands-aws-strategic-collaboration-with-a-us-1-5-billion-plus-agreement-to-scale-its-unified-enterprise-ai-data-stack" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;iTWire (ClickHouse)&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://itwire.com/guest-articles/company-news/cyberagents-exchange-to-recognise-defenders-building-agentic-solutions-that-improve-ai-security" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;iTWire (CyberAgents)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


3. Động lực Ngành, Doanh nghiệp &amp;amp; Hệ sinh thái
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Quỹ Đầu Tư Toàn Cầu Hội Tụ Tại Sydney Tìm Kiếm Cơ Hội Ở Các Hãng Lượng Tử Úc&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Quantum Australia đang kết nối nhiều nhà đầu tư quốc tế tại Sydney—gồm Quỹ Novo Nordisk (Đan Mạch), ACTAI Ventures (Mỹ), Serendipity Capital (Singapore) và Playground Global (Thung lũng Silicon)—nhằm mở rộng nguồn vốn thương mại hoá cho các doanh nghiệp công nghệ lượng tử Úc. Song song, Ủy ban châu Âu đề xuất quỹ vốn chủ quyền trị giá 50 tỷ EUR (khoảng 82,3 tỷ AUD) trong dự thảo Đạo luật Lượng tử nhằm giảm phụ thuộc hạ tầng tính toán bên ngoài.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Nguồn trích dẫn:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href="https://techday.com.au/story/global-investors-eye-australian-quantum-firms-in-sydney" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TechDay Australia&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://itbrief.com.au/story/global-investors-eye-australian-quantum-firms-in-sydney" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ITBrief Australia&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.innovationaus.com/europe-plans-e50b-sovereign-equity-fund-for-quantum-tech/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;InnovationAus&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Số Lỗ Hổng Bảo Mật Do AI Phát Hiện Tăng Gấp Ba Trong Năm 2026&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Nghiên cứu mới từ Trung tâm An ninh và Công nghệ Mới nổi (CSET) thuộc Đại học Georgetown cho thấy số lỗ hổng bảo mật (CVE) công bố hằng tháng đã tăng gấp ba lần từ tháng 1 đến tháng 9/2026 khi các mô hình như Claude Mythos xuất hiện, kéo theo tỷ lệ hệ thống chậm vá lỗi và thời gian xử lý sự cố gia tăng. Dù thiệt hại tài chính thực tế từ ransomware chưa tăng đột biến tương ứng, giới chuyên gia khuyến nghị doanh nghiệp Úc khẩn trương siết chặt quy trình vá lỗi.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Nguồn trích dẫn:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href="https://techday.com.au/story/the-ai-cyber-apocalypse-is-running-late-but-that-s-no-reason-to-relax" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TechDay Australia&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://itbrief.com.au/story/the-ai-cyber-apocalypse-is-running-late-but-that-s-no-reason-to-relax" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ITBrief Australia&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Tranh Luận Địa Chính Trị Về Mô Hình Trọng Số Mở &amp;amp; Giá Trị Của Câu Chuyện Thương Hiệu&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Việc hội đồng quản trị Nvidia phục chức CEO Jensen Huang sau thời gian ngắn từ nhiệm tiếp tục làm nóng cuộc tranh luận về vai trò của mô hình AI trọng số mở (open-weight) giữa Mỹ và Trung Quốc. Ở góc độ doanh nghiệp, giới tiếp thị tại Úc nhận định khi AI tạo sinh giúp bất kỳ đội ngũ nào cũng có thể sản xuất nội dung hàng loạt, tư duy chiến lược và nghệ thuật kể chuyện thương hiệu lại trở thành lợi thế khan hiếm nhất.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Nguồn trích dẫn:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.innovationaus.com/huangs-return-brings-open-weight-ai-debate-to-forefront/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;InnovationAus&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://techday.com.au/story/when-anyone-can-build-almost-anything-with-ai-story-is-the-scarce-skill" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TechDay Australia&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;68% Tổ Chức APAC Đẩy Mạnh AI Chủ Quyền; SAS Được Vinh Danh Nhóm Dẫn Đầu IDC MarketScape&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Báo cáo &lt;em&gt;IDC MarketScape: Asia/Pacific Sovereign AI Platform 2026&lt;/em&gt; ghi nhận AI chủ quyền đã chuyển từ thảo luận chính sách sang mua sắm thực tế, với &lt;strong&gt;68%&lt;/strong&gt; tổ chức tại châu Á–Thái Bình Dương đang triển khai hoặc có kế hoạch áp dụng trong 12 tháng tới; nền tảng &lt;em&gt;SAS Viya&lt;/em&gt; được xếp vào nhóm Dẫn đầu nhờ khả năng kiểm soát dữ liệu, mô hình và quản trị theo vùng.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Nguồn trích dẫn:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;a href="https://itwire.com/guest-articles/company-news/sas-named-a-leader-in-idc-marketscape-for-asia-pacific-sovereign-ai-platform" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;iTWire&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




