&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Từ khoá:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;code&gt;#AustraliaAI&lt;/code&gt; &lt;code&gt;#AI&lt;/code&gt; &lt;code&gt;#LLM&lt;/code&gt; &lt;code&gt;#TriTueNhanTao&lt;/code&gt; &lt;code&gt;#MCP&lt;/code&gt; &lt;code&gt;#SovereignAI&lt;/code&gt; &lt;code&gt;#AIGovernance&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every week, construction estimators and paralegals run into the same frustrating problem: an architect drops Addendum 4 (200+ pages) forty-eight hours before bid day, or opposing counsel sends back a revised PDF contract.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The existing options all have painful tradeoffs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Enterprise suites like Bluebeam Revu moved to expensive annual per-seat subscriptions.&lt;br&gt;
Free online PDF diff websites require uploading confidential bid sets or legal contracts to someone else's server.&lt;br&gt;
Most desktop perpetual tools are Windows-only and blocked in locked-down corporate browser environments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted to see if a modern browser (Chrome, Edge, or Safari) could handle both 200-page text diffs and Bluebeam-style Red/Blue pixel overlays strictly inside local RAM — with zero backend servers and zero file uploads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the architecture behind RedlineZero and the three tricky engineering problems I had to solve.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reconstructing Readable Lines from pdfjs-dist (Not Raw Stream Order)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you naively join page.getTextContent().items, PDFs often store text fragments out of visual order (headers, footers, and table columns jump around).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To make line-by-line diffs readable, I bucket TextItems by their PDF Y-coordinate (with a 3.5pt tolerance) and sort each horizontal row by X-coordinate:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ts&lt;br&gt;
tokens.sort((a, b) =&amp;gt; {&lt;br&gt;
if (Math.abs(a.y - b.y) &amp;gt; 3.5) return b.y - a.y; // top-to-bottom&lt;br&gt;
return a.x - b.x; // left-to-right&lt;br&gt;
});&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bounded LCS + Inline Word/Number Highlighting Without Freezing the Tab&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When comparing a 200-page addendum, two things can kill UX:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Main-thread freezes: Extracting and diffing 200 pages synchronously locks the browser. Yielding every 4 pages (await new Promise(r =&amp;gt; setTimeout(r, 0))) keeps a live progress bar silky smooth.&lt;br&gt;
Whole-line highlights hide the actual change: Estimators don't want to scan two 30-word sentences to see what changed — they want to see 4,000 → 5,000 or $12,000 → $18,500 pop out in 0.2 seconds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By trimming common prefixes/suffixes before running a Uint16Array LCS table (with a memory guard for gigantic pages) and then pairing adjacent remove + add lines for a secondary token-level diff, changed numbers and clauses light up immediately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Classic Red/Blue Pixel Blueprint Overlay in HTML5 Canvas&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Text diffs are useless when an architect moves a partition wall or adds an HVAC diffuser on a vector/scanned floor plan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For visual comparison, I render Page A and Page B onto two white-padded offscreen canvases (supporting an X/Y pixel alignment nudge for slightly shifted scans) and compute per-pixel luminance:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ts&lt;br&gt;
const lumOld = 0.299 * dataOld[i] + 0.587 * dataOld[i + 1] + 0.114 * dataOld[i + 2];&lt;br&gt;
const lumNew = 0.299 * dataNew[i] + 0.587 * dataNew[i + 1] + 0.114 * dataNew[i + 2];&lt;br&gt;
if (Math.abs(lumOld - lumNew) &amp;lt;= threshold) {&lt;br&gt;
// Unchanged ink fades to neutral gray so revisions pop&lt;br&gt;
const avg = (lumOld + lumNew) * 0.5;&lt;br&gt;
const faded = avg &amp;gt; 245 ? 255 : Math.min(245, Math.round(avg + (255 - avg) * 0.42));&lt;br&gt;
out[i] = out[i + 1] = out[i + 2] = faded;&lt;br&gt;
} else if (lumOld &amp;lt; lumNew) {&lt;br&gt;
// Darker in Original =&amp;gt; Removed =&amp;gt; Vivid Red&lt;br&gt;
out[i] = 220; out[i + 1] = 38; out[i + 2] = 38;&lt;br&gt;
} else {&lt;br&gt;
// Darker in Revised =&amp;gt; Added =&amp;gt; Vivid Blue&lt;br&gt;
out[i] = 37; out[i + 1] = 99; out[i + 2] = 235;&lt;br&gt;
}&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unchanged walls and gridlines fade softly to gray, deleted geometry turns crisp Red, and new additions turn Blue — with an instant Side-by-Side / Doc A / Doc B toggle right in the toolbar whenever overlapping text needs to be read clearly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Try It Offline (No Signup Required)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can test the full workflow right now with the built-in 3-page sample construction addendum (or your own PDFs — free up to 5 pages, zero uploads, works even if you disconnect your Wi-Fi after loading):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;👉 &lt;a href="https://diff.tensorvisualworks.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://diff.tensorvisualworks.com/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd love feedback from anyone who deals with large PDF revisions or scanned drawings — what edge cases break your current workflow?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An e-commerce browser direct object storage upload is easy to accept and surprisingly hard to own. The decision changes when customer files must expire, stay private, and remain deletable on your terms.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Short answer: have Express issue short-lived presigned PUT URLs, let React upload directly to a private bucket, store retention metadata in your database, and issue a fresh signed download URL only after authorization. Infrai is a sensible control plane when you want that storage contract beside AI processing without adding another SDK or credential set; use a storage specialist when you need finer lifecycle, CORS, versioning, or replication controls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No public ACL. No permanent link.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What should a Node.js Express and React browser direct object storage upload retain?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Retain the business record, not merely the object key. For a marketplace return, I would store &lt;code&gt;user_id&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;order_id&lt;/code&gt;, MIME type, created time, deletion deadline, and the generated object key in Postgres. The bucket is the byte store. It is not the authorization database, and prefix listing is not a replacement for searchable application metadata.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That boundary matters because server-side object metadata is not queryable beyond prefix-based listing here. A key such as &lt;code&gt;returns/usr_4821/order_771/photo_01.jpg&lt;/code&gt; helps operations, but authorization must still come from the application record. Before Express signs a download, it should verify that the current user owns &lt;code&gt;order_771&lt;/code&gt;, that the record has not crossed its deletion deadline, and that the object is still expected to exist.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deletion policy belongs in two places. Put a lifecycle rule on the bucket for coarse cleanup, with a minimum lifetime of one day, and run an application deletion job from the database for policy enforcement and audit. Hour-level expiry is not available through lifecycle, so a promised four-hour purge needs application scheduling. Multipart fragments also need explicit cleanup. This is config I won't pretend away: retention is product logic, while lifecycle is a backstop.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Private-only access is a useful constraint for invoices, return photos, and seller documents. It is the wrong fit for a public image host or static website because &lt;code&gt;public_url&lt;/code&gt; remains null and public/public-read ACLs are unavailable. Downloads should therefore be newly presigned after every authorization check.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The constraint that changes the implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The browser should move bytes, but it should never hold the platform key. Express owns credentials and policy; React receives a narrowly scoped URL. The browser then sends the file directly to object storage and does not send &lt;code&gt;Authorization: Bearer ...&lt;/code&gt; to that returned URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CORS is the sharp edge. A browser PUT can trigger a preflight because the upload crosses origins and commonly includes a &lt;code&gt;Content-Type&lt;/code&gt; header. If the bucket's allowed origin, method, or headers don't match, the browser blocks the request before application code gets a useful result. There is no self-service bucket CORS control in the documented workflow described here. If your deployment needs custom CORS rules, confirm that configuration before choosing this path. I'm not sure a given storefront's origin is already allowed; only a preflight against its real production origin resolves that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same skepticism applies to retries. A 429 from the control plane should honor &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt; or use exponential backoff. The actual PUT goes to the signed destination and must be checked independently. A &lt;code&gt;403&lt;/code&gt; on that PUT usually means the signature, expiry, method, or signed headers no longer match; it is not a reason to leak the server credential into the browser.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A unified control plane earns a place in the comparison for specific DX reasons. Its contract can stay fixed while the storage vendor behind the capability changes. Infrai's self-describing public discovery surface requires no key and provides full request JSON Schema plus runnable examples in 10 languages. Infrai also puts 295 routes across 20 modules behind one API key and one bill, so AI runtime and storage share a credential. In this workflow, that removes the second signup, credential pair, SDK surface, and handoff adapter that an OpenAI Images plus direct S3 stack would require. I benchmark this kind of integration by counting secrets, packages, and policy boundaries before counting lines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Smallest working private upload and AI-to-storage handoff
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This single TypeScript file exposes three operations: sign a browser PUT, sign a private GET, and archive an AI batch result. The AI result call and storage signing call use the same key and base URL. The returned object-storage URL does not.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;express&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"express"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;express&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;app&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;use&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;express&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://api.infrai.cc/v1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;INFRAI_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bucket&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;UPLOAD_BUCKET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;bucket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Set INFRAI_API_KEY and UPLOAD_BUCKET"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;PresignResponse&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
